 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "Mali rose in the upper Niger valley after the fall of Ghana.\nFounding: Sundiata Keita, a Mandinka prince, defeated Sumanguru (Soumaoro Kanté), king of Sosso, at the battle of Kirina in 1235, and founded the empire. The capital was Niani.\nMansa Musa (ruled about 1312–1337) was its most famous ruler. On his pilgrimage to Mecca in 1324 he gave away so much gold in Cairo that its value fell. He built mosques and made Timbuktu a centre of Islamic learning (the Sankore mosque and university).\nSources of wealth: gold from Bambuk and Bure, the salt trade across the Sahara, taxes on trade, farming.\nGovernment: the Mansa (emperor) ruled with governors in the provinces and a strong army.\nDecline (15th century): weak rulers, quarrels over succession, revolts, and attacks by the Tuareg and the Songhai; Songhai took over.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "a": 1235,
   "b": 1324
  },
  "problem": "How many years passed between the battle of Kirina ({a}) and Mansa Musa's pilgrimage ({b})?",
  "steps": [
   "Both years are AD, so subtract the earlier from the later.",
   "{b} − {a} = {b - a} years."
  ],
  "answer": "{b - a} years",
  "checks": []
 },
